we have another pair of committee  members for our final presentation julia  chasman is a veteran film producer with  a dozen feature credits who's been a  studio executive at Showtime Universal  and industry entertainment currently she  runs her own home design company which  designs and renovates home in the Los  Angeles area she has served on the  nickel Executive Committee for the last  six years and has been a judge since her  acceptance to the Academy in 2007  Daniel Petrie jr. is an Academy Award  nominee for his screenplay Beverly Hills  Cop he is a past president of the  Writers Guild of America West and  current president of the Writers Guild  foundation he is also a former governor  of the Academy and a longtime member of  the Academy's nickel fellowships  committee presenting the award to Walker  McKnight R Julia chasman and Daniel  Petrie Junior  [Laughter]  street rat alley as you gather from that  beautifully performed scene leads a  small band of teenage girls in their  makeshift existence in the wildly  original ramshackle hellish city of the  future where they struggle to survive as  the eldest of the girls alley is charged  with finding food shelter and keeping up  the hopes of her younger charges with no  means of support and only their rickety  skateboards to make their way these  latter day heirs to the pickpockets of  Oliver Twist must fend off daily attacks  by boys BOTS and sadistic multi limbed  futuristic creatures as they scramble  for food alley not only keeps them fed  she keeps up their spirits and serves as  a mother to the orphan girls although  she's not much older than they are okay  the bombed out city where they live was  once one of the great ones was it Paris  New York San Francisco we have to wait  until the last train pulls out at the  very end of the story to find out Walker  McKnight has created a uniquely  terrifying and fabulous post-apocalyptic  city of the future as he put it himself  in his artists statement a fun dystopia  populated by multi limbed freakish  creatures and robots but the main  characters are all beautifully realized  young women and the spirit of the script  is undeniable full of action drama humor  heart and humanism it's a unique  combination for the sci-fi genre a  native of Atlanta Georgia Walker  McKnight didn't start writing seriously  until he was thirty the time has been  well spent though as the assurance of  his years shows in his command of his  craft and a voice which is all his own  please help me welcome to the stage 2019  nickel fellow Walker McKnight  [Applause]  [Laughter]  this is a major phobia of mine I'm about  to have a panic attack so I think I'm  gonna read this whole page in about 15  seconds so just hold on  thank you Dan and Julia for the  incredibly kind words  thanks to Gian Don nickel for the gift  of this competition and the academy for  putting so much effort and so many  resources into it for so many years  thanks to Joan Y and Chris Carr Chi and  the nickel staff for doing the hard work  of running the fellowship thanks to  Jennifer yuh Nelson and the nickel  committee for taking time out of their  wonderful careers to help those of us  trying to learn this craft it means a  lot and thanks to the other fellows and  finalists for being such fun and  supportive partners in this process I  also have to thank the great  screenwriting teachers I've had because  they've been huge my first teachers  Jenna Millie and Michael lucker my  professor Jack Boozer from GSU and Tim  Alba who has been a big friend and  supporter these people have had to read  my worst scripts and it probably took  years off their lives to do so I'm sure  - but like great teachers somehow they  were still able to encourage me to keep  going and I think if any one of them had  said something different than that I I  might not have thanked you two rows and  a mandolin and Wes and Tyreese for that  incredible read I never expected to hear  dialogue that I've written spoken by  anyone other than the voices in my own  head and there are a lot of those you're  much better than those voices that was  incredible and thank you Geetha for for  directing that thanks for my brother  Brian for being a wonderful friend and  movie watching partner all my life but  above all above  all thanks to my parents Wanda and  Walker who fed my love of fantasy and  sci-fi from my earliest memories to  reading to me and indulging my constant  requests to bring home VHS movie rentals  with monsters in them they have  continued to encourage their child in  this crazy career path long long after  he's passed an age that any child should  need to be encouraged in a career path  so I can't thank them enough and I love  you as Julia mentioned I really didn't  start writing until age 30 I have no  idea what I was doing before that just  staring at a wall I guess with a with a  relatively late start like that comes a  pretty healthy dose of imposter syndrome  I've often felt like I was trespassing  on somebody else's career field  something that was meant really for only  people who just knew it was their  calling since childhood  or high school or college or even their  20s and so for 15 years my imposter  syndrome it's been like a constant  roommate and and lately honestly it's  really started to feel more like the  landlord really a really terrible when  banging on the door and shouting at all  hours  thanks to Nikol I now have something to  shout back and that's a really wonderful  thing to close I just want to say that  good stories are the most magical thing  in the world to me  my favorite storytellers throughout my  life have been able to conjure something  out of nothing and make me actually care  and the difficulty of accomplishing that  never ceases to blow my mind this is the  craft that I'm trying to learn and if  I'm ever able to bring even just a tiny  fraction of the thrill to somebody that  my favorite storytellers brought to me  then I'll be happy thank you  [Applause]
 

